[tool] d-06 post_finding {"claim": "The indirect-detection status of the about 1.1 TeV thermal higgsino, taken from abstracts only: it is not excluded by current gamma-ray searches, and
[finding] f-011 The indirect-detection status of the about 1.1 TeV thermal higgsino, taken from abstracts only: it is not excluded by current gamma-ray searches, and the result
**Finding:** The indirect-detection status of the about 1.1 TeV thermal higgsino, taken from abstracts only: it is not excluded by current gamma-ray searches, and the result depends on the halo profile. (a) arXiv:2405.13104 (Rodd, Safdi, Xu, PRD 110, 043003, 2024) ran a 15-yr Fermi-LAT line-like search that comes 'within an order one factor' of the sensitivity needed to detect the higgsino, for some Milky Way analog profiles. In that analysis H.E.S.S. is subleading to Fermi for the higgsino. (b) arXiv:2210.03140 (Montanari, Moulin, Rodd, PRD 107, 043028) finds the higgsino 'out of reach by at least a factor of a few' even at the ultimate sensitivity of current H.E.S.S.-like IACTs. (c) arXiv:2608.07234 (H.E.S.S. Inner Galaxy Survey, 546 h, DOI 10.1103/d8tj-55kc, dated 2026 and unverified) quotes a line limit of 2.3e-28 cm^3/s at 1 TeV for an Einasto profile and states that thermal higgsino DM is 'probed for DM Milky Way models' for the first time. The abstract does not say whether it excludes the higgsino, or for which profiles.
[tool] tc-261016_1-w2-d-06-78 -> ok: Recorded finding f-011.
[tool] d-06 post_finding {"claim": "CTAO is the decisive future test. arXiv:2008.00692 (Rinchiuso et al., PRD 103, 023011) finds that CTA 'has the potential to become the first experime
[finding] f-012 CTAO is the decisive future test. arXiv:2008.00692 (Rinchiuso et al., PRD 103, 023011) finds that CTA 'has the potential to become the first experiment that has
**Finding:** CTAO is the decisive future test. arXiv:2008.00692 (Rinchiuso et al., PRD 103, 023011) finds that CTA 'has the potential to become the first experiment that has sensitivity to the thermal' higgsino. arXiv:2405.13104 finds that CTA and SWGO could detect the line-like signal of the 1.1 TeV higgsino 'at high significance... for all but the most pessimistic DM profiles'. arXiv:2506.08084 (PoS ICRC2025) projects that CTAO-North, observing at large zenith angles, could reach the required sensitivity within the next decade. The server's indirect-detection treatment (p-wave, mass-splitting suppressed, sigma_v of about 1e-41 cm^3/s, f-002) is SUPERSEDED. A higgsino annihilates through an s-wave, Sommerfeld-enhanced channel, and the literature treats the thermal target as near the current Fermi/IACT reach rather than 15 orders of magnitude below it.
[tool] tc-261016_1-w2-d-06-79 -> ok: Recorded finding f-012.
