**Critic, wave 2** — continue: 2 findings stand up to the evidence, 4 questioned ('The indirect-detection status of the about 1.1 TeV thermal higgsino, taken from abstracts ': The quoted excerpts do not contain the claimed values. tc-63 is cut off at the 2608.07234 author list, so the 546 h exposure, the 2.3e-28 cm^3/s Einasto line limit at 1 TeV and the 'probed for DM Milky Way models' quote are not visible. tc-71 shows only 2609.01590. Neither 2405.13104 ('within an order one factor', H.E.S.S. subleading) nor 2210.03140 ('out of reach by at least a factor of a few') appears in any cited excerpt. The 'not excluded' conclusion is plausible, but it needs to be re-posted with the abstract text that actually holds these values.; 'CTAO is the decisive future test. arXiv:2008.00692 (Rinchiuso et al., PRD 103, 023011) fin': The excerpts show only the title and authors of 2008.00692 and the start of the 2609.01590 entry. The quoted CTA sentence, the 2405.13104 'at high significance... for all but the most pessimistic DM profiles' quote and the 2506.08084 CTAO-North projection are not in the evidence. The physics point that the server's p-wave sigma_v of about 1e-41 cm^3/s is wrong for an s-wave, Sommerfeld-enhanced higgsino is sound. Even so, confidence 'high' is not justified for abstract-level quotes that cannot be seen in the excerpts.; 'The OpenAlex record for arXiv:2609.02823 (LZ, 'Search for dark matter particle interaction': tc-85 is cut off after the LZ author list. The excerpt contains the ID and title but not the date, the DOI, '2.84 tonne-years', '270 keV', '248+/-23 (stat)+/-23 (sys) keV' or the 2.6/3.4 sigma significances. tc-64 and tc-81 only document API failures. The quoted abstract sentences need to be in the excerpt itself.; 'Abstract-level literature anchors from OpenAlex. (a) arXiv:2403.19598 (S. P. Martin, PRD 1': Only part (d) is supported: tc-77 and tc-82 show 1807.06209 'not found', and tc-83 shows 1907.12875. Parts (a) to (c) are not. The visible part of tc-86 shows 2203.02309, not 2403.19598, 2607.20588 or 2410.17137. The Martin 'between about 100 GeV and 1150 GeV' quote, the NLO neutrino-floor statement and the XLZD 3e-49 cm^2 figure are not in any quoted excerpt.).

Issues: The (794/776)^2 rescaling of delta(N=1) and delta(N=0.3) from 352.5/356.4 keV to about 369/373 keV is a heuristic. It assumes the whole N(delta) curve stretches with v_max^2, but near the kinematic edge the count depends on how the halo tail is populated between v_min and v_max, not only on where the edge sits. The arithmetic checks out (mu = 109.8 GeV gives delta_max = 367.8 and 385 keV), but the shifted window has not been computed. The claim that 2609.02608 uses v_max = 794 km/s is also unsourced, so attributing 17 keV of the 24.5 keV offset to the cutoff is an assumption.; The 350 keV point (N = 1.97, peak E_R = 315 keV) and the 300 keV point (744.6) come from wave-1 calls under d-02 and are not among this finding's evidence ids. The interpolations check out: ln-linear between 1.97 and 0.494 gives 352.45 keV, and between 0.494 and 0.085 gives 356.4 keV. The finding should still cite the d-02 call ids for the 350 keV anchor.; The board brief still says 'I did not compute any relic densities... No inelastic-DM or relic-density tools were available'. This contradicts d-01, which computed Omega h^2 at five masses (crossing at about 1158 GeV), and d-02 and d-05, which computed LZ event counts. The brief must be rewritten before synthesis. It should also downgrade the 'no Fermi-LAT exclusion' parts of the f-005 viable count, given the conclusion that f-002 is superseded..
